; Shared PWeb application-identity include (CAP-6b3 anti-fork
; extraction of the neutral half of tools/setup/pwebprovgate.issi).
; EVERY install profile consumes this one file:
;
;   tools/setup/normal.iss  Evergreen Bootstrapper       (CAP-6b1)
;   tools/setup/offline.iss Evergreen Standalone         (CAP-6b2)
;   tools/setup/fixed.iss   bundled Fixed Version Runtime (CAP-6b3)
;
; so the installed application identity - the AppId, the app name, the
; per-user install location, the privilege level, the architecture
; gating and the unchanged CAP-6 release triple - can never fork
; between profiles. A profile that changed any of it would install a
; DIFFERENT application over the same users, which is exactly what the
; shared AppId forbids.
;
; What each profile still owns:
;   PWEB_PROFILE      profile name, stamped into VersionInfoDescription
;                     AND into the HKCU profile marker below, so both
;                     the binary and the installed machine say which
;                     profile it is
;   PWEB_SETUP_BASENAME  the profile's own setup basename
;   its own [Files]   the profile-specific payload (a provisioning
;                     installer + helper, or the bundled runtime tree)
;   its own [Code]    the profile-specific gate, if any
;
; The CAP-6 release triple itself moved out at CAP-6b4, into
; tools/setup/pwebapppayload.issi, so each profile can #include it at
; the POSITION its own [Files] order needs (the fixed profile includes
; it AFTER its verdict gate, which is what makes a failed switch leave
; the source install byte-identical). It is still authored exactly
; once, and every profile still consumes that one file.
;
; Defines consumed here (all derived by the profile build scripts):
;   PWEB_PAYLOAD_DIR  staged payload directory holding the release
;                     triple; required, no default - a hand-run ISCC
;                     must fail loudly rather than build a partial
;                     setup out of whatever happens to be on disk
;   PWEB_APP_VERSION  optional, defaults to the historical 0.1.0
;   PWEB_PROFILE      REQUIRED and validated (CAP-6b4): exactly one of
;                     normal | offline | fixed-runtime, case-sensitive.
;                     There is deliberately NO default: the value is
;                     written to the machine as the profile marker, and
;                     a silent default would mislabel an install
;   PWEB_SETUP_BASENAME  REQUIRED (CAP-6b4): the output basename. No
;                     default, and in particular never "setup" - every
;                     executable named setup.exe is shimmed by Windows
;                     application compatibility into loading extra DLLs
;                     (version.dll and friends) from its own directory
;   PWEB_COMPRESSION  optional, defaults to the historical lzma2
;   PWEB_SOLID        optional, defaults to the historical yes
;
; The two compression overrides exist for ONE reason: the CAP-6b3
; fixed profile embeds a ~690 MB runtime tree, where the ratified
; normal/offline settings turn a build into an unbounded wall-clock
; cost. They default to today's values, so the CAP-6b1 and CAP-6b2
; setups compile with byte-identical [Setup] semantics; only a profile
; that explicitly overrides them gets anything else.
;
; Nothing here can reach the network: there is no URL, no download
; primitive and no [Run] entry in this file, and the payload the
; profiles embed is the only source a target machine ever sees.
;
; CAP-6b4 profile marker. The three profiles are mutually exclusive
; MODES of ONE product, so the machine gets ONE record of which mode is
; installed. It is written here, once, from PWEB_PROFILE, by Inno's own
; [Registry] phase - which runs after every [Files] entry, therefore
; after the fixed profile's verdict gate, so the marker is the COMMIT
; POINT of a profile switch: a setup that aborts anywhere earlier
; leaves the marker reading the SOURCE profile. Uninstall removes it
; (uninsdeletevalue) and then the two key levels if they are empty
; (uninsdeletekeyifempty), so an uninstall leaves no trace of the mode
; either.
;
; WHAT IT IS, precisely. It is an INSTALL RECORD - for migration (a
; future installer can know what it is replacing), for diagnostics, and
; for the CAP-6b4 profile matrix, which reads it as the authoritative
; answer to "which mode is installed" on every row. It is deliberately
; NOT read by the shipped application at run time: the fixed-mode host
; finds its bundled tree by absolute path and validates it, and the
; Evergreen-mode host never looks for a tree at all, so neither needs
; to be told which one it is. Nothing about runtime behaviour depends
; on this value, and nothing should be made to depend on it without
; ratifying that as a change.
;
; It is also NOT the ownership signal that gates the stale-tree
; reclaim. That signal is the presence of our own uninstaller in {app}
; (see PWebOwnsInstallDir in tools/setup/pwebprovgate.issi), because
; the marker is per-USER and says nothing about which DIRECTORY setup
; was pointed at - and because the very install the reclaim exists to
; migrate, a pre-CAP-6b4 fixed install, predates the marker and carries
; none.
;
; It is never written to app.pwb, to JS, to localStorage or to the CWD,
; and the mode is never INFERRED from "the runtime tree exists" - a
; tree can be orphaned, a marker cannot.

#ifndef PWEB_PAYLOAD_DIR
  #error PWEB_PAYLOAD_DIR must be defined - build via a profile build script (test/cap6b1/build_normal_setup.ps1, test/cap6b2/build_offline_setup.ps1 or test/cap6b3/build_fixed_setup.ps1)
#endif
#ifndef PWEB_APP_VERSION
  #define PWEB_APP_VERSION "0.1.0"
#endif
; profile identity: every profile emits a setup with the SAME [Setup]
; identity, so the binary itself must say WHICH profile it is
; (VersionInfoDescription below) and the machine must record it (the
; [Registry] marker below). Each .iss defines PWEB_PROFILE; CAP-6b4
; removed the historical "normal" default, because a default here would
; silently STAMP a wrong mode onto a user's machine.
#ifndef PWEB_PROFILE
  #error PWEB_PROFILE must be defined by the including profile (.iss) - exactly one of normal | offline | fixed-runtime; there is deliberately no default
#endif
; the ratified profile-name set, authored once. Pos() is case-SENSITIVE
; (measured against the pinned ISCC 6.7.3, unlike ISPP's = / != string
; operators), so this rejects case drift too: the marker value must be
; byte-exact, not merely recognizable.
#define PWEB_PROFILE_SET "|normal|offline|fixed-runtime|"
#if Pos("|" + PWEB_PROFILE + "|", PWEB_PROFILE_SET) == 0
  #error PWEB_PROFILE must be exactly one of normal | offline | fixed-runtime (case-sensitive)
#endif
#ifndef PWEB_SETUP_BASENAME
  #error PWEB_SETUP_BASENAME must be defined by the including profile (.iss) - and never "setup": setup.exe is appcompat-shimmed into unsafe DLL loading
#endif
; and it must not merely be PRESENT - it must not be the shimmed name.
; An absence guard alone would let /DPWEB_SETUP_BASENAME=setup compile
; cleanly and emit exactly the setup.exe this shard exists to prevent.
; LowerCase() folds the comparison, so SETUP / Setup / sEtUp are all
; refused; the Pos() form mirrors the PWEB_PROFILE guard above.
#if Pos("|" + LowerCase(PWEB_SETUP_BASENAME) + "|", "|setup|") != 0
  #error PWEB_SETUP_BASENAME must not be "setup" (in any casing): every executable named setup.exe is shimmed by Windows application compatibility into loading version.dll and other DLLs from its own directory
#endif
#ifndef PWEB_COMPRESSION
  #define PWEB_COMPRESSION "lzma2"
#endif
#ifndef PWEB_SOLID
  #define PWEB_SOLID "yes"
#endif
; the fixed profile's installed runtime subdirectory, authored ONCE
; here: tools/setup/fixed.iss deploys the tree INTO it, and
; tools/setup/pwebprovgate.issi reclaims it with [InstallDelete] when
; an Evergreen profile is installed over a fixed one. Two literals
; would be two directories, and the orphan this reclaim exists to kill
; would come straight back.
;
; The PARENT is authored beside it because Inno only removes what an
; install RECORDED: the Evergreen profiles never create {app}\runtime,
; so reclaiming only the leaf would leave that intermediate directory
; behind forever - and every gate enumerates with -Recurse -File, so
; nothing would ever see it. The reclaim therefore names both, and the
; CAP-6b4 contract asserts the subdir really does live under the parent.
#define PWEB_RUNTIME_PARENT "runtime"
#define PWEB_RUNTIME_SUBDIR "runtime\webview2"
; the profile marker: one per-user key, one value, authored once
#define PWEB_MARKER_PARENT "Software\PWeb"
#define PWEB_MARKER_KEY "Software\PWeb\PWebRelease"
#define PWEB_MARKER_VALUE "Profile"

[Setup]
AppId={{7C3E9A1B-5D24-4F68-A0C9-2B8E6D4F1A57}
AppName=PWeb Release
AppVersion={#PWEB_APP_VERSION}
AppPublisher=PWeb
VersionInfoDescription=PWeb Release Setup ({#PWEB_PROFILE})
DefaultDirName={localappdata}\Programs\PWebRelease
; per-user by construction: never any elevation, never any attempt to
; force where Microsoft places the runtime
PrivilegesRequired=lowest
ArchitecturesAllowed=x64compatible
ArchitecturesInstallIn64BitMode=x64compatible
DisableProgramGroupPage=yes
Compression={#PWEB_COMPRESSION}
SolidCompression={#PWEB_SOLID}
OutputBaseFilename={#PWEB_SETUP_BASENAME}
Uninstallable=yes

[Registry]
; THE PROFILE MARKER - the single machine-readable fact that says which
; of the three mutually exclusive modes is installed, and the COMMIT
; POINT of a profile switch (Inno runs [Registry] after every [Files]
; entry, so a setup that aborts earlier never reaches it and the marker
; still names the SOURCE profile).
;
; Uninstall order is the reverse of install order, so the value goes
; first and the two key levels follow only while they are empty - an
; uninstall therefore removes the marker without ever touching a
; neighbouring key some other PWeb product might own.
;
; This is per-user (HKCU) by construction, matching
; PrivilegesRequired=lowest: nothing here needs or asks for elevation.
Root: HKCU; Subkey: "{#PWEB_MARKER_PARENT}"; Flags: uninsdeletekeyifempty
Root: HKCU; Subkey: "{#PWEB_MARKER_KEY}"; Flags: uninsdeletekeyifempty
Root: HKCU; Subkey: "{#PWEB_MARKER_KEY}"; ValueType: string; \
    ValueName: "{#PWEB_MARKER_VALUE}"; ValueData: "{#PWEB_PROFILE}"; \
    Flags: uninsdeletevalue
